    </nav>
    <div class="study-hero-copy">
      <p class="eyebrow" data-i18n="eyebrow">Until He Comes · Bible Study · Revelation 8–13</p>
      <h1 data-i18n="title">Faithful Witness &amp; Unseen Victory</h1>
      <p class="study-subtitle" data-i18n="subtitle">Keep bearing witness to Jesus through opposition. Our victory rests in the Lamb.</p>
    </div>
    <blockquote class="hero-scripture">
      <p lang="en" data-i18n="heroVerse" data-i18n-lang>They overcame him by the blood of the Lamb and by the word of their testimony. They stayed faithful even at the cost of their lives.</p>
      <cite lang="en" data-i18n="heroCitation" data-i18n-lang>Revelation 12:11 · study paraphrase</cite>
    </blockquote>
  </header>

  <main class="study-content">
    <aside class="study-aside" aria-label="Study summary" data-i18n-aria-label="summaryAria">
      <div><span class="aside-label" data-i18n="themeLabel">Theme</span><p data-i18n="theme">The living Christ calls us to bear witness through opposition, discern what demands our worship, and hold fast to him.</p></div>
      <div><span class="aside-label" data-i18n="keyLabel">Key texts</span><p data-i18n="keyTexts">Revelation 6:9–10 and 7:9–12 · The martyrs’ prayer and the multitude’s worship<br />Revelation 8:3–5, 9:20–21, and 10:9–11 · Prayer, repentance, and the scroll<br />Revelation 11:3, 11–15 · The witnesses and Christ’s reign<br />Revelation 12:9–12 and 13:7–17 · The Lamb’s victory and the saints’ endurance</p></div>
      <div><span class="aside-label" data-i18n="timeLabel">Time</span><p data-i18n="time">40 minutes</p></div>
      <div><span class="aside-label" data-i18n="presentationLabel">Canva presentation</span><a href="https://canva.link/88n5ugek6usfsoa" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" data-i18n="presentationLink">Open the slides</a></div>
      <div><span class="aside-label" data-i18n="serviceLabel">Sunday service</span><p data-i18n="serviceTime">Sermon segment in the recording: 00:52:51–01:57:37</p></div>
      <div><span class="aside-label" data-i18n="facebookLabel">Facebook Live</span><a href={facebookVideo} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" data-i18n="facebookLink">Watch the November 22 service on Facebook</a></div>
    </aside>

    <article class="study-article">
      <section aria-labelledby="reading-title">
        <p class="section-number">01</p>
        <h2 id="reading-title" data-i18n="readingTitle">Read Revelation with our eyes on Christ</h2>
        <p data-i18n="reading1">Pay attention to where John takes us. At the end of chapter 11, heaven announces Christ’s reign. In chapter 12, the vision takes us back to the birth of the child the dragon fears. That return helps us see the same age from another angle.</p>
        <p data-i18n="reading2">We call this progressive parallelism or recapitulation. In the seals, trumpets, and bowls, Revelation revisits the age between Jesus’ first coming and his return, with judgments growing more severe. Compare the authority given over a fourth of the earth in Revelation 6:8 with the trumpets that strike a third of the sea, fresh water, and lights in the sky. In chapter 6, the martyrs cry out, “How long?” In chapter 7, God seals his people and shows us a multitude worshiping before the Lamb. He holds his people through their suffering.</p>
        <div class="discussion-prompt"><span data-i18n="discuss">Discuss</span><p data-i18n="readingQuestion">Read Revelation 6:9–10 alongside 7:9–12. How do these two scenes help us pray when justice seems slow in coming?</p></div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="trumpets-title">
        <p class="section-number">02</p>
        <h2 id="trumpets-title" data-i18n="trumpetsTitle">God hears our prayers</h2>
        <blockquote class="verse-card">
          <p lang="en" data-i18n="trumpetsVerse" data-i18n-lang>The smoke of the incense rose before God with the prayers of the saints. Then fire from the altar was thrown to earth.</p>
          <cite lang="en" data-i18n="trumpetsCitation" data-i18n-lang>Revelation 8:3–5 · study paraphrase</cite>
        </blockquote>
        <p data-i18n="trumpets1">When the Lamb opens the seventh seal, heaven falls silent. The prayers of the saints rise before God with the incense. From that same altar, fire is thrown to earth. We see God responding to the cry of his people. Even prayers that seem to go unheard matter to him.</p>
        <p data-i18n="trumpets2">The first four trumpets strike land, sea, fresh water, and sky. With the fifth and sixth, the abyss opens and cavalry is released from the Euphrates. The plagues in Egypt help us recognize these as warnings. Yet Revelation 9:20–21 tells us that many still clung to their idols and sins. When God exposes what we are holding on to, let us ask him for hearts willing to repent.</p>
        <div class="discussion-prompt"><span data-i18n="discuss">Discuss</span><p data-i18n="trumpetsQuestion">What does Revelation 9:20–21 show us about a heart that has grown used to clinging to other things? Where do we need to respond to something God has already made clear?</p></div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="witness-title">
        <p class="section-number">03</p>
        <h2 id="witness-title" data-i18n="witnessTitle">Bear witness even when it costs us</h2>
        <p data-i18n="witness1">In Revelation 10, John eats the little scroll before he is told to prophesy again. It is sweet in his mouth and bitter in his stomach. The good news of salvation is sweet. The message we carry also weighs on us because sin and judgment are real, and following Jesus can bring opposition. Let us speak honestly about the hope he gives us through the troubles we still face. Jesus invites us to walk with him and rely on him as we face those troubles.</p>
        <p data-i18n="witness2">The two witnesses prophesy in sackcloth. When they finish their testimony, the beast kills them and the world celebrates. God breathes life into them before their enemies. At the seventh trumpet, heaven announces that Christ will reign forever. Some readers understand the witnesses to be two particular people. We can also see in them the calling of the whole church to keep sharing the good news of Jesus and entrust the outcome to him.</p>
        <p data-i18n="witness3">I was told that when I was about three, I would run around at the front during worship and cling to my dad. Today it is by God’s grace that I get to share his Word. Let us pray for the children in our church as they grow up. God can use their lives for his glory in ways we cannot yet see.</p>
        <div class="discussion-prompt"><span data-i18n="discuss">Discuss</span><p data-i18n="witnessQuestion">Which part of the good news is hardest for us to say when people may resist it? How can we speak truthfully and with care?</p></div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="conflict-title">
        <p class="section-number">04</p>
        <h2 id="conflict-title" data-i18n="conflictTitle">Victory rests on the blood of the Lamb</h2>
        <p data-i18n="conflict1">In Revelation 12, a woman is about to give birth, and the dragon waits to devour her child. The child is taken safely to God, and the dragon is thrown down. Behind the opposition God’s people face, we see the anger of a defeated enemy who knows his time is short.</p>
        <p data-i18n="conflict2">The dragon still pursues those who keep God’s commands and hold to the testimony of Jesus. In Revelation 12:11, their victory rests on the blood of the Lamb, and they hold to their testimony even when it costs their lives. When we are weary, remember that Jesus gave his life and rose again. What he has done gives us strength to endure.</p>
        <div class="discussion-prompt"><span data-i18n="discuss">Discuss</span><p data-i18n="conflictQuestion">How does Revelation 12:11 change the way we see a situation where faithfulness to Jesus feels like defeat?</p></div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="allegiance-title">
        <p class="section-number">05</p>
        <h2 id="allegiance-title" data-i18n="allegianceTitle">Discern what demands our worship</h2>
        <blockquote class="verse-card">
          <p lang="en" data-i18n="allegianceVerse" data-i18n-lang>This calls for the endurance and faith of the saints.</p>
          <cite lang="en" data-i18n="allegianceCitation" data-i18n-lang>Revelation 13:10 · study paraphrase</cite>
        </blockquote>
        <p data-i18n="allegiance1">In Revelation 13, the dragon uses the beast from the sea to demand worship and wage war against the saints. Pay attention to the words “it was allowed.” Its power has limits. The beast from the earth looks like a lamb and speaks like a dragon. It directs worship to the first beast and uses even buying and selling to pressure people.</p>
        <p data-i18n="allegiance2">In Revelation 7, God seals the people who belong to him. In Revelation 13, the beast imposes its own mark. Let us face the question these images put before us. Who will have our allegiance when giving it comes at a cost? Pay attention to promises that sound good while asking for the obedience that belongs to Christ.</p>
        <div class="discussion-prompt"><span data-i18n="discuss">Discuss</span><p data-i18n="allegianceQuestion">Where do we feel pressure to set aside obedience to Christ for income, acceptance, or comfort? How can we help one another stay faithful?</p></div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="eternity-title">
        <p class="section-number">06</p>
        <h2 id="eternity-title" data-i18n="eternityTitle">Live in the light of eternity</h2>
        <p data-i18n="eternity1">Think of one small wave in a vast ocean. A moment is that small beside eternity, yet God is fully present in every moment of our lives. Second Peter 3:8 reminds us that with the Lord one day is like a thousand years. The love he has shown us through Jesus reaches through every moment. Because Christ is alive, we can cling to him as we age, face illness, or grow weary.</p>
        <p data-i18n="eternity2">Eternal life is knowing God and being with him forever. As our achievements and possessions pass away, Christ is at work shaping who we are so that we reflect his glory. Read Revelation 7:9–12 and see people from every nation worshiping before the Lamb. In their cry, we hear the testimony of people who have learned that following Jesus is worth it. Salvation belongs to God and to the Lamb. By his grace we are saved through faith in him. Let us surrender our lives to him again.</p>
        <blockquote class="verse-card" cite="https://www.derekprince.com/books/rules-of-engagement">
          <p lang="en" data-i18n="characterQuote" data-i18n-lang>God is more concerned with our character than with our achievements. Achievements have importance only in the realm of time. Character is eternal. It determines what we will be through eternity.</p>
          <cite lang="en" data-i18n="characterCitation" data-i18n-lang>Derek Prince, <i lang="en">Rules of Engagement</i></cite>
        </blockquote>
        <p data-i18n="eternity3">Read Romans 5:3–5 as well. Through suffering, God teaches us to endure. Endurance forms our character, and tested character produces hope. God is doing something in us as he teaches us to stay faithful.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="response-section" aria-labelledby="response-title">
        <p class="section-number" data-i18n="responseSection">07 · Put it into practice</p>
        <h2 id="response-title" data-i18n="responseTitle">Take one step of faithfulness this week</h2>
        <ol class="response-steps">
          <li><span data-i18n="respond">Respond</span><p data-i18n="step1">Read Revelation 8:3–5. Bring God one situation where you have been waiting for justice or help. Pray for someone going through the same struggle.</p></li>
          <li><span data-i18n="respond">Respond</span><p data-i18n="step2">Choose one person you can tell this week about the hope Jesus has given you. Speak honestly and make time to listen to what they are going through.</p></li>
          <li><span data-i18n="respond">Respond</span><p data-i18n="step3">Name one compromise you are facing. Pray about it with another believer and decide on one concrete act of obedience to Jesus.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section class="closing-prayer" aria-labelledby="prayer-title">
        <p class="section-number" data-i18n="prayerSection">08 · Pray</p>
        <h2 id="prayer-title" data-i18n="prayerTitle">Lord Jesus, help us stay faithful</h2>
        <p data-i18n="prayer">Lord Jesus, thank you for hearing our prayers and giving your life for us. Forgive us for the times we have wanted acceptance and comfort more than we wanted to obey you. Give us courage to bear witness and wisdom to recognize deception. Keep shaping our character so others can see your goodness. We surrender our lives to you. Keep us faithful as we wait for your return. Amen.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="takeaway" aria-label="Study takeaway" data-i18n-aria-label="takeawayAria">
        <span data-i18n="takeawayLabel">Take this with you</span>
        <p data-i18n="takeaway">The Lamb has won. By his grace, we can stay faithful and bear witness until he comes.</p>
      </section>

